WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.002
Adam Driver, welcome to our program.

00:00:02.002 --> 00:00:02.669
Thank you.

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:03.461
Thank you very much.

00:00:03.461 --> 00:00:04.379
Thanks for having me.

00:00:04.379 --> 00:00:05.463
How difficult was it

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:07.007
for you to play Enzo?

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:08.341
What about him?

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:09.009
Well, I mean,

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:11.261
I feel the pressure with every job.

00:00:11.261 --> 00:00:12.929
This one was,

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:13.722
you know,

00:00:13.722 --> 00:00:16.474
because he was such an Italian icon,

00:00:16.474 --> 00:00:17.600
an automotive

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.810
automotive icon,

00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.813
but largely because it was Michael.

00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:23.523
What I liked about the character

00:00:23.523 --> 00:00:24.941
is that Michael's version of him

00:00:24.941 --> 00:00:27.068
was that he was a racer first.

00:00:27.068 --> 00:00:29.320
So he was calm on the surface,

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:30.780
but had a constant engine

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:31.906
going on underneath.

00:00:31.906 --> 00:00:33.241
And what I love about

00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:34.534
Michael in his films

00:00:34.534 --> 00:00:34.909
is that

00:00:34.909 --> 00:00:36.077
I feel like they're accessible

00:00:36.077 --> 00:00:39.956
to everyone.

00:00:39.998 --> 00:00:46.254
If you get into one of my cars,

00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:49.090
you get in the wind,

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:50.216
but they're not dumbed down

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:51.176
where he insults

00:00:51.176 --> 00:00:52.510
his audience intelligence

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:53.970
by telling them everything.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.389
You have to you do have to do work

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:57.599
as an audience member.

00:00:57.599 --> 00:00:58.725
It's not necessarily

00:00:58.725 --> 00:00:59.768
a character,

00:00:59.768 --> 00:01:00.351
a character

00:01:00.351 --> 00:01:02.562
that's easily accessible or likable,

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:04.314
which I think is more true

00:01:04.314 --> 00:01:05.815
to how things are in life.

00:01:05.815 --> 00:01:08.443
So and and and just this

00:01:08.443 --> 00:01:10.278
these moments in his films that are just,

00:01:10.278 --> 00:01:11.738
you know, incredible cinema,

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:13.364
just beautiful, moving images.

00:01:13.364 --> 00:01:15.241
Well, that's that's for damn sure.

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:16.659
It's a very beautiful look.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:18.161
And Michael Michael Mann,

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:19.245
you're talking about the director

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:20.413
just for our audience.

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:21.206
I just before

00:01:21.206 --> 00:01:22.540
I get to one of these pieces

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.375
of beautiful cinema and a clip,

00:01:24.375 --> 00:01:26.294
I want to ask you.

00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:27.629
What is it about Michael Mann

00:01:27.629 --> 00:01:28.546
just for our audience?

00:01:28.546 --> 00:01:29.631
Tell us, what are the films?

00:01:29.631 --> 00:01:30.882
What is it about what he does

00:01:30.882 --> 00:01:31.758
you've told us about

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:32.967
not dumbing down

00:01:32.967 --> 00:01:35.345
and really making the audience work

00:01:35.345 --> 00:01:36.846
and you love his genre.

00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:38.223
Just give us a few that

00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:39.224
that you really liked

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:41.226
and made you wanted to do this.

00:01:41.226 --> 00:01:42.227
Well, I've actually,

00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:43.937
after working with him, have gone

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:44.938
when I went back

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:45.688
when we were shooting

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:46.481
and watch his films,

00:01:46.481 --> 00:01:48.066
I see Michael in all his films.

00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:52.529
I see him in Heat and The Insider and

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.364
Last of The Mohicans and Thief

00:01:54.364 --> 00:01:55.865
and the Common Thread.

00:01:55.865 --> 00:01:56.407
I would,

00:01:56.407 --> 00:01:59.410
I would say largely external, either

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:02.413
a lot about process and obsession

00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:03.998
and commitment,

00:02:03.998 --> 00:02:06.042
speaking generally in big themes.

00:02:06.042 --> 00:02:09.045
But as an actor, I was surprised

00:02:09.045 --> 00:02:10.755
at how much Michael is interested

00:02:10.755 --> 00:02:12.173
in internal life.

00:02:12.173 --> 00:02:13.383
In almost 90% of

00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:15.135
his notes are internal life.

00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:16.678
He doesn't

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:18.680
waste time with a lot of characters

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:19.597
coming on film

00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:21.724
and explaining everything that they feel,

00:02:21.724 --> 00:02:23.560
which I feel is often

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:24.936
you kind of get in scripts

00:02:24.936 --> 00:02:26.980
is you can't, you can't guess.

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:28.064
There's no ambiguity.

00:02:28.064 --> 00:02:29.107
You have to,

00:02:29.107 --> 00:02:30.233
as a character, say

00:02:30.233 --> 00:02:31.776
everything that you feel.

00:02:31.776 --> 00:02:32.527
And I think that's

00:02:32.527 --> 00:02:33.945
why his movies are so rich

00:02:33.945 --> 00:02:36.281
and he has such

00:02:36.281 --> 00:02:38.908
a sharp attention to detail.

00:02:38.908 --> 00:02:39.951
And he is

00:02:39.951 --> 00:02:41.244
relentless in this

00:02:41.244 --> 00:02:43.454
in a quest for authenticity.

00:02:43.454 --> 00:02:46.416
Tell me what sort of scenes here

00:02:46.416 --> 00:02:47.083
match that?

00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:50.086
I mean, you know, really the authenticity

00:02:50.295 --> 00:02:53.006
knowing without actually telling you

00:02:53.006 --> 00:02:55.425
in terms of this film.

00:02:55.508 --> 00:02:57.844
Or in this film there

00:02:57.844 --> 00:03:00.430
the engine sounds is something that

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:04.517
he is very particular about.

00:03:04.601 --> 00:03:05.643
A lot of

00:03:05.643 --> 00:03:08.688
some of the cars were the real thing.

00:03:08.688 --> 00:03:09.814
And he would

00:03:09.814 --> 00:03:12.817
you know, these are like $70,000,050.50

00:03:12.942 --> 00:03:15.862
$50 million cars that were shipped there

00:03:15.862 --> 00:03:17.947
and nine mikes were strapped to them

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:19.115
and driven down the tunnel

00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:20.950
because you can't replicate the engine.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.869
You kind of have to have the real thing.

00:03:22.869 --> 00:03:25.038
So even in watching it, you you get the

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:25.997
we can't use the

00:03:25.997 --> 00:03:28.666
the engine sounds of the other cars

00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:29.918
that were crashing in the film.

00:03:29.918 --> 00:03:32.128
So that kind of specificity

00:03:32.128 --> 00:03:33.504
and not just recording it,

00:03:33.504 --> 00:03:35.131
sitting idle on a

00:03:35.131 --> 00:03:36.007
on a tarmac,

00:03:36.007 --> 00:03:37.425
actually taking it down a tunnel

00:03:37.425 --> 00:03:38.384
so you can get the sound

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.886
bouncing off the buildings is

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:41.679
is a perfect example in our movie.

00:03:41.679 --> 00:03:42.555
That's amazing.

00:03:42.555 --> 00:03:44.390
And you yourself are known

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:46.142
to be equally passionate

00:03:46.142 --> 00:03:47.602
about getting things right,

00:03:47.602 --> 00:03:50.021
about immersing yourself in roles.

00:03:50.021 --> 00:03:51.564
I don't know. Are you method?

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:54.234
How do you describe what you do

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:55.443
and how you get

00:03:55.443 --> 00:03:58.071
totally into the characters?

00:03:58.154 --> 00:04:00.031
I you know, for me it's kind of like

00:04:00.031 --> 00:04:01.574
it's it's not method.

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:02.325
It's,

00:04:02.325 --> 00:04:02.825
you know, it's

00:04:02.825 --> 00:04:04.911
kind of like moving to a new country.

00:04:04.911 --> 00:04:07.455
And I felt this way about theater.

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:08.581
You move to a new country

00:04:08.581 --> 00:04:10.166
and you have to adjust to the time.

00:04:10.166 --> 00:04:11.334
So if you're in London,

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:11.960
I mean, here

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:13.753
if I went to London at the time,

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:15.672
our differences 5 hours

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:16.839
for the first couple of days,

00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:19.008
it would feel like I feel tired.

00:04:19.008 --> 00:04:20.260
But then after a few days,

00:04:20.260 --> 00:04:22.262
just naturally by osmosis,

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:24.305
you just start to adjust to the time

00:04:24.305 --> 00:04:26.057
and it becomes natural.

00:04:26.057 --> 00:04:27.558
The same thing with the character.

00:04:27.558 --> 00:04:29.560
I load up on as much information

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.144
as I can,

00:04:30.144 --> 00:04:31.896
really to just control anxiety

00:04:31.896 --> 00:04:33.356
as much as possible.

00:04:33.356 --> 00:04:35.316
So I feel like there's not a stone

00:04:35.316 --> 00:04:36.609
that I haven't overturned.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.194
And then you move to a country

00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:39.570
which is usually a film,

00:04:39.570 --> 00:04:40.488
and then it's awkward

00:04:40.488 --> 00:04:41.489
for a couple of days

00:04:41.489 --> 00:04:42.615
and then just naturally

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:43.866
from wearing the costumes

00:04:43.866 --> 00:04:44.784
or being on set

00:04:44.784 --> 00:04:45.702
or the other actors

00:04:45.702 --> 00:04:47.412
or piece of

00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:49.205
technical thing or direction

00:04:49.205 --> 00:04:50.623
that Michael has given you.

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:53.960
Then suddenly just being in that person's

00:04:54.043 --> 00:04:55.420
shoes literally for,

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:56.671
you know, 14 hours a day

00:04:56.671 --> 00:04:57.630
for months on end,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.465
naturally, you just kind of adjust

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.634
your body, adjust your mindset, adjust.

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:02.176
And it's

00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:03.594
that's my favorite part of acting.

00:05:03.594 --> 00:05:06.597
It's, you know, forced empathy for,

00:05:06.723 --> 00:05:07.849
you know, three or four months,

00:05:07.849 --> 00:05:08.683
sometimes a year,

00:05:08.683 --> 00:05:08.891
if you're

00:05:08.891 --> 00:05:11.436
including prep in your in your life,

00:05:11.436 --> 00:05:12.687
you know.

00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:13.688
So that for me

00:05:13.688 --> 00:05:14.647
is, yeah, it's not

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:17.483
it's kind of unconscious in a way.

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:20.320
So let's get back to the actual scenes.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:21.821
You said something

00:05:21.821 --> 00:05:22.905
a while back about

00:05:22.905 --> 00:05:24.949
how Enzo is all these complex

00:05:24.949 --> 00:05:26.075
different characteristics,

00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:28.619
including not necessarily likable.

00:05:28.619 --> 00:05:29.746
And I actually found that

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:31.122
to be very true.

00:05:31.122 --> 00:05:33.916
And I thought that was part of the

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:35.960
I thought that was part of

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.337
what was compelling

00:05:37.337 --> 00:05:38.463
as well as other things.

00:05:38.463 --> 00:05:41.049
So I'm going to play a little clip.

00:05:41.049 --> 00:05:42.592
This is not on the race course.

00:05:42.592 --> 00:05:44.552
It's actually interaction

00:05:44.552 --> 00:05:46.346
between you and Penelope Cruz,

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:49.390
who plays your wife, Laura, and she is

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:50.892
now going at you

00:05:50.892 --> 00:05:53.519
over the death of your son.

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:55.855
You were supposed to save him.

00:05:55.938 --> 00:05:57.357
You blame me for his death?

00:05:57.357 --> 00:05:58.900
Yes. Yes.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.736
Because you promised me you wouldn't die.

00:06:01.736 --> 00:06:03.488
Everything I did.

00:06:03.488 --> 00:06:04.906
Everything.

00:06:04.906 --> 00:06:07.075
Table showing what calories he could eat,

00:06:07.075 --> 00:06:08.951
what went in, what came out.

00:06:08.951 --> 00:06:11.162
I graph the degrees of albuminuria,

00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:12.330
the degrees of anxiety.

00:06:12.330 --> 00:06:14.665
Mia diuresis.

00:06:14.665 --> 00:06:16.167
I know more about nephritis

00:06:16.167 --> 00:06:17.835
and dystrophy than cars.

00:06:17.835 --> 00:06:18.669
Yes, I blame you.

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:20.713
I gave you because you let him die.

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:24.258
The father deluded himself.

00:06:24.342 --> 00:06:26.594
The great engineer.

00:06:26.594 --> 00:06:28.096
I will restore my son.

00:06:28.096 --> 00:06:30.807
12th wealth doctors, Italian doctors.

00:06:30.807 --> 00:06:32.975
But I could not.

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:35.103
I did not.

00:06:35.103 --> 00:06:37.355
Is so raw and is so dramatic.

00:06:37.355 --> 00:06:38.106
And.

00:06:38.106 --> 00:06:40.149
And to see him as the,

00:06:40.149 --> 00:06:41.025
you know, the race

00:06:41.025 --> 00:06:42.610
car expert

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:43.569
and then getting into this

00:06:43.569 --> 00:06:45.863
very personal situation.

00:06:45.863 --> 00:06:46.739
Half the story

00:06:46.739 --> 00:06:48.366
or maybe the most of the film

00:06:48.366 --> 00:06:50.243
is about his personal relationship

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:51.202
with his wife,

00:06:51.202 --> 00:06:52.453
who's his business partner

00:06:52.453 --> 00:06:54.580
with his lover, who's the married,

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:57.375
the mother of his next son.

00:06:57.375 --> 00:07:00.128
It's very, very personal.

00:07:00.128 --> 00:07:00.336
Yeah.

00:07:00.336 --> 00:07:01.212
Which is another thing

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:02.338
that I could say about what

00:07:02.338 --> 00:07:04.257
I love about Michael's films.

00:07:04.257 --> 00:07:05.508
And I probably should say

00:07:05.508 --> 00:07:06.426
just also hedge

00:07:06.426 --> 00:07:09.053
people's expectations that it very easily

00:07:09.053 --> 00:07:10.471
could have been a movie

00:07:10.471 --> 00:07:11.556
with a loose plot.

00:07:11.556 --> 00:07:12.890
That's an excuse to look at.

00:07:12.890 --> 00:07:14.517
Beautiful old Ferrari

00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:17.186
driving at a beautiful time,

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.730
you know, in the world.

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:20.231
You know,

00:07:20.231 --> 00:07:23.025
the late fifties in Italy is beautiful.

00:07:23.025 --> 00:07:24.735
The costumes are beautiful.

00:07:24.735 --> 00:07:27.447
But Michael's films are character driven.

00:07:27.447 --> 00:07:31.075
He's he is committed and obsessed with

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:32.827
having three dimensional characters.

00:07:32.827 --> 00:07:33.369
So hopefully

00:07:33.369 --> 00:07:34.996
by the time something does happen

00:07:34.996 --> 00:07:38.332
that you actually care, which seems to be

00:07:38.416 --> 00:07:39.709
is honestly hard

00:07:39.709 --> 00:07:41.002
to find in a lot of scripts.

00:07:41.002 --> 00:07:42.712
This is a character driven movie.

00:07:42.712 --> 00:07:43.629
First,

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:47.008
where the spectacle is, is I'm

00:07:47.008 --> 00:07:49.218
avoiding all driving metaphors, but

00:07:49.218 --> 00:07:53.055
it's easy to say it takes a backseat

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:56.225
to to what his his character study is.

00:07:56.225 --> 00:07:58.311
And

00:07:58.311 --> 00:08:02.315
and in a way they they all they

00:08:02.398 --> 00:08:03.733
communicate with each other.

00:08:03.733 --> 00:08:04.901
You know,

00:08:04.901 --> 00:08:06.569
who cares if anyone crashes

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:07.570
if you don't really.

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:09.947
Ah, don't care about the people.

00:08:09.947 --> 00:08:10.781
I want to ask you

00:08:10.781 --> 00:08:12.450
because I always get surprised

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:15.661
when I think that you as a younger man

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.163
joined the Marines.

00:08:17.163 --> 00:08:18.456
And I wonder

00:08:18.456 --> 00:08:21.959
whether that gives you the intensity,

00:08:21.959 --> 00:08:22.752
the discipline,

00:08:22.752 --> 00:08:25.755
the connection with with raw reality

00:08:25.755 --> 00:08:26.547
that you were able

00:08:26.547 --> 00:08:27.924
to bring with you to Hollywood

00:08:27.924 --> 00:08:30.593
and this profession?

00:08:30.676 --> 00:08:31.010
I don't know.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:32.929
I haven't analyzed it

00:08:32.929 --> 00:08:36.140
very much other than I mean, apart of

00:08:36.224 --> 00:08:37.808
as far as what intensity

00:08:37.808 --> 00:08:41.479
I bring to perform, I will say that I'm

00:08:41.562 --> 00:08:42.897
I look at a film set

00:08:42.897 --> 00:08:45.316
no different than I did a gun team.

00:08:45.316 --> 00:08:46.609
Obviously, the

00:08:46.609 --> 00:08:49.445
the stakes are life and death in a

00:08:49.445 --> 00:08:50.321
in the Marine Corps.

00:08:50.321 --> 00:08:51.614
Whereas in the civilian world

00:08:51.614 --> 00:08:52.240
or acting,

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:53.199
you're pretending that

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:54.700
they're life and death.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.161
But the way the process of working on it

00:08:57.161 --> 00:08:59.247
is, is is almost the exact same.

00:08:59.247 --> 00:08:59.622
You know, it's

00:08:59.622 --> 00:09:00.456
a group of people

00:09:00.456 --> 00:09:02.166
trying to accomplish a mission

00:09:02.166 --> 00:09:04.085
that's bigger than any one person.

00:09:04.085 --> 00:09:04.418
You know,

00:09:04.418 --> 00:09:05.336
you have a role

00:09:05.336 --> 00:09:07.004
that is a part of the whole piece.

00:09:07.004 --> 00:09:08.172
It's not about you.

00:09:08.172 --> 00:09:10.299
And the better that you do your job,

00:09:10.299 --> 00:09:11.551
it allows someone else

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:12.385
to do their job

00:09:12.385 --> 00:09:13.886
to the best that they can.

00:09:13.886 --> 00:09:15.012
You're battling,

00:09:15.012 --> 00:09:16.973
you know, it's improvization.

00:09:16.973 --> 00:09:17.974
There's a lot of pressure.

00:09:17.974 --> 00:09:19.267
You're battling the elements.

00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:21.602
You're you're battling time

00:09:21.602 --> 00:09:22.979
and when under that

00:09:22.979 --> 00:09:24.188
kind of intense pressure

00:09:24.188 --> 00:09:27.191
and scrutiny, you you are in a

00:09:27.191 --> 00:09:29.360
you have friendships and dynamics

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:31.195
that it's hard to explain to people

00:09:31.195 --> 00:09:31.988
who are not there.

00:09:31.988 --> 00:09:32.572
You know,

00:09:32.572 --> 00:09:33.406
the crossover

00:09:33.406 --> 00:09:35.324
between the military and acting,

00:09:35.324 --> 00:09:37.493
especially as a group of people

00:09:37.493 --> 00:09:38.286
doing this thing

00:09:38.286 --> 00:09:40.288
that's bigger than any one person

00:09:40.288 --> 00:09:41.706
really made sense to me.

00:09:41.706 --> 00:09:43.332
And so I don't take it for granted.

00:09:43.332 --> 00:09:46.377
I films of being in Indiana

00:09:46.377 --> 00:09:47.920
could not have been more of

00:09:47.920 --> 00:09:49.422
of science fiction,

00:09:49.422 --> 00:09:50.881
that I would be a part of that world.

00:09:50.881 --> 00:09:52.216
I had no connection to anyone

00:09:52.216 --> 00:09:54.760
in the acting industry

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.762
and might as well have been an astronaut.

00:09:56.762 --> 00:09:59.140
So I don't take it for granted

00:09:59.140 --> 00:10:01.934
and have been the beneficiary of movies

00:10:01.934 --> 00:10:03.603
that have given me images and moments

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:05.062
that will last my entire life.

00:10:05.062 --> 00:10:06.063
So when we're shooting,

00:10:06.063 --> 00:10:07.898
I don't take it lightly.

00:10:07.898 --> 00:10:10.318
I think it's an incredible opportunity.

00:10:10.318 --> 00:10:11.110
It's a document

00:10:11.110 --> 00:10:13.237
that's going to last forever

00:10:13.237 --> 00:10:15.406
that has the potential of finding

00:10:15.406 --> 00:10:17.199
someone that is in a culture

00:10:17.199 --> 00:10:18.659
that has nothing to do with mine

00:10:18.659 --> 00:10:21.287
and hopefully would articulate a feeling

00:10:21.287 --> 00:10:22.538
or give a similar moment

00:10:22.538 --> 00:10:23.414
that will live with them

00:10:23.414 --> 00:10:24.665
for the rest of their life.

00:10:24.665 --> 00:10:25.124
What what

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:26.709
an amazing thing to be a part of.

00:10:26.709 --> 00:10:27.668
I don't want to mess it up.

